            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent cholecystitis and cholelithiasis in a gallbladder remnant 14 years after a converted cholecystectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>332</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2484/rcr.v5i1.332</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 52-year-old man presented to the emergency department with a one-day history of epigastric pain. The patient reported a remote history of a &quot;difficult&quot; laparoscopic cholecystectomy that was converted to an open cholecystectomy in 1994. Further operative details were unavailable. Multiple radiologic studies were obtained, all demonstrating a saccular cystic structure in the gallbladder fossa containing calculi. A completion open cholecystectomy, or &quot;recholecystectomy,&quot; revealed a remnant gallbladder with cholecystitis and cholelithiasis. Multimodality imaging findings are reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
